Relaxation with Starsmasher \

\f1\b0 \ulnone 1. sph.input file:\
	- TF is the number of time units for which to run the relaxation \
	- The code unit of time is a combination of G,M,R and for a solar mass star is about 30 min\
	- The NNOPT parameter should be about sqrt(N) \
	- TRELAX should be about an oscillation period of the star. Th magnitudeML of it controls the drag force (proportional to v/TRELAX). \
	- equal mass controls the mass of the particles 0 means there\'92s equal mass densities as a function of r, 1 means all particles have the same mass. \
2. How to relax a star:\
	-Copy over the sph.init, sph.input, submit_best.sh and src files from another relaxation. \
	- Copy the asc stellar file (in the case of the FB dataset)\
	- run the commands above \
	- cd into src and run \'93make\'94. This should create an executable, and now you can run things. \
\

\f0\b \ul SMX \

\f1\b0 \ulnone - To run SMX just type \'94SMX\'94 and a pop-up should appear. \
	- If my local smx doesn\'92t work, use \'93~jll839/bin/smx\'94\
- To plot energies, run the command \'93v 0\'94 \
- To plot general properties, run the command \'93pa 0000\'94 where the number is your snapshot 
\f0\b \ul \

\f1\b0 \ulnone 	- In the bottom right panel, green curve is for gravitational acceleration and blue curve for hydrodynamic acceleration. Equilibrium is reached once they are equal and opposite. \
	- If the curve in the top left panel is flat, it means you are in a convective regime\
	- h is the smoothing length. The kernel for each particle is 2h.\
	- Horizontal spreads in some plots at later times will be due to the slight motions of particles from their original configurations. \
- parent.sph file has the properties of the original profile. \
- to find the source file, look in home directory \'93~/.sm_macros/default\'94\

